# SYNOPSIS

```usage
sha1sum [-c] [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`sha1sum` reads each <file>, or standard input when there is none or for a <file> of `-`, and prints its SHA-1 digest, 160 bits as 40 lower-case hexadecimal digits, then two spaces and the file's name, `<stdin>` for standard input.

: `-c`
  Check instead: each <file>, or standard input, is a list of lines as `sha1sum` prints them, a digest, a space, then a space or `*`, then a file's name.
  `sha1sum` reads each file listed and prints its name and `: OK` or `: FAILED` on standard output.
: `-b`, `-t`
  Accepted and ignored, for binary and text modes, which are the same.

`sha1sum` is not in POSIX.
It is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/sha1sum`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/sha1sum`.
There is no native `sha1sum`.

# SEE ALSO

sha224sum(1), sha256sum(1), md5sum(1), cksum(1), namespace(6); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/sha1sum.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

Messages go to standard error, after the name `sha1sum` was run by.
A <file> that cannot be opened is reported as `sha1sum: open` <file>`:` and the error, one that cannot be read as <file>`: read error:` and the error, and the next is read.
With `-c`, a listed file that cannot be read is reported the same way, and at the end `sha1sum` says how many lines were `improperly formatted`, how many `listed files could not be read` and how many `computed checksums did NOT match`, if any.
An unknown flag prints the usage message.
The exit status is 1 if anything failed or did not match, otherwise 0.

# BUGS

`-b` and `-t` work but are left out of the usage message.

With `-c`, a listed name of `-` is a file called `-`, not standard input, and a name ends at the first carriage return.

Untested on VectraOS: `tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs `sha256sum` alone of the digest commands.
